WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.022
What does the residue measure?

00:00:02.022 --> 00:00:05.883
Look at omega squared divided by omega minus x.

00:00:05.883 --> 00:00:08.293
Only the denominator vanishes at x.

00:00:08.293 --> 00:00:13.946
For this simple pole, multiply by omega minus x and let omega approach x.

00:00:13.946 --> 00:00:16.313
The remaining numerator tends to x squared.

00:00:16.313 --> 00:00:19.363
At x equal to one half, that gives one quarter.

00:00:19.363 --> 00:00:22.947
This is the coefficient of one over omega minus x.

00:00:23.247 --> 00:00:25.244
Blend two points at the same angle.

00:00:25.244 --> 00:00:26.630
Fix the angle t.

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:30.768
Mark the point on the small circle and the matching point on the ellipse.

00:00:30.768 --> 00:00:34.544
The first term takes one minus lambda times the circle point.

00:00:34.544 --> 00:00:37.295
The second takes lambda times the ellipse point.

00:00:37.295 --> 00:00:40.388
At lambda zero, the sum gives the circle point.

00:00:40.388 --> 00:00:42.499
At one half, it is their midpoint.

00:00:42.499 --> 00:00:44.803
At one, it reaches the ellipse point.

00:00:44.803 --> 00:00:46.210
Now let t turn.

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:49.090
The blended point traces the loop called C lambda.

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.472
Keep the pole inside every loop.

00:00:51.472 --> 00:00:53.712
Each intermediate loop is an ellipse.

00:00:53.712 --> 00:00:55.524
Look at its two real tips.

00:00:55.524 --> 00:00:59.556
The left one stays to the left of x, and the right one stays to the right.

00:00:59.556 --> 00:01:04.974
As t increases through one turn, every loop goes counterclockwise once around the pole.

00:01:04.974 --> 00:01:12.142
Cauchy's formula therefore gives the same normalized integral, one quarter, for every lambda from zero to one.

00:01:12.442 --> 00:01:14.673
The tangent supplies the differential.

00:01:14.673 --> 00:01:17.574
Follow the point upward at the right tip of the ellipse.

00:01:17.574 --> 00:01:19.728
At the top the tangent points left.

00:01:19.728 --> 00:01:21.882
Advance the angle by a small amount.

00:01:21.882 --> 00:01:25.978
The short chord follows the tangent more closely as the step shrinks.

00:01:25.978 --> 00:01:31.097
The differential is the tangent, C prime of t, times the angle step, d t.

00:01:31.097 --> 00:01:34.062
Both its direction and its speed enter the integral.

00:01:34.362 --> 00:01:36.551
Multiply the two complex arrows.

00:01:36.551 --> 00:01:41.692
The first arrow represents the quotient, omega squared over omega minus x.

00:01:41.692 --> 00:01:44.529
The second is the tangent at the moving point.

00:01:44.529 --> 00:01:49.200
Complex multiplication adds their angles and multiplies their lengths.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:56.261
Dividing by two pi i turns the product clockwise by a quarter turn and scales it by one over two pi.

00:01:56.261 --> 00:01:59.140
Multiply this rate by the small angle step.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:01.722
Put the resulting arrow at the tip of the running sum.

00:02:02.022 --> 00:02:04.595
On the small circle, the factors cancel.

00:02:04.595 --> 00:02:08.541
The radius arrow from x is epsilon times e to the i t.

00:02:08.541 --> 00:02:11.164
The tangent is i times that same arrow.

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:13.873
Divide the tangent by the radius arrow.

00:02:13.873 --> 00:02:16.965
Their lengths cancel, and the quarter turn leaves i.

00:02:16.965 --> 00:02:19.760
That i cancels the i in the normalization.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:24.133
What remains is the circle value squared, times d t over two pi.

00:02:24.433 --> 00:02:27.219
Keep the constant and turn the other two waves.

00:02:27.219 --> 00:02:28.563
Expand the square.

00:02:28.563 --> 00:02:32.722
The constant arrow has length x squared, one quarter, and points right.

00:02:32.722 --> 00:02:37.628
The next term has length two x epsilon, also one quarter, and turns once.

00:02:37.628 --> 00:02:42.065
The last has length epsilon squared, one sixteenth, and turns twice.

00:02:42.065 --> 00:02:46.608
Each rotating arrow meets an equal opposite arrow during its own cycle.

00:02:46.608 --> 00:02:48.591
Both full-turn averages vanish.

00:02:48.591 --> 00:02:50.297
Only the constant survives.

00:02:50.297 --> 00:02:53.049
Its average over the full circle is one quarter.

00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:55.453
Build the partial integral from zero.

00:02:55.453 --> 00:03:00.167
A sub C of T adds the little arrows from angle zero to angle T.

00:03:00.167 --> 00:03:04.135
Before the first step, the interval is empty, so the sum is zero.

00:03:04.135 --> 00:03:07.803
Halfway around the small circle the real part is one eighth.

00:03:07.803 --> 00:03:10.747
The imaginary part is one over four pi.

00:03:10.747 --> 00:03:12.410
The integral is still moving.

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:13.497
Finish the turn.

00:03:13.497 --> 00:03:18.063
The imaginary contributions cancel and the real part reaches one quarter.

00:03:18.363 --> 00:03:21.405
The two signed heights give one complex integral.

00:03:21.405 --> 00:03:22.791
Return to the ellipse.

00:03:22.791 --> 00:03:28.764
Read the real part of the rate as height on the first boundary and the imaginary part on the second.

00:03:28.764 --> 00:03:33.840
Each small angle interval contributes its signed height times its angular width.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:38.747
Watch those two contributions move the running sum horizontally and vertically.

00:03:38.747 --> 00:03:42.757
The ellipse and the small circle take different routes in the value plane.

00:03:42.757 --> 00:03:45.402
Both finish at one quarter on the real axis.

